ernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0. Node1 uses the signed RHEL AppStream vsftpd 3.0.5-12 package, and lifecycle observations confirm the release and kernel remained unchanged.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9951ef4dc4dc4dd2","cmd-c4d497cd3f6643b8","cmd-5e6692d8724949ed","cmd-cca9e98d60e64c40","cmd-be0b80416a4044f9","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.82,"summary":"Final hygiene is good: no failed units, package health remained sound, bounded temporary artifacts were removed, and the only new listener is the required node1 FTP service. Two unnecessary, bounded configuration mutations occurred: seccomp_sandbox=YES was added in the initial configuration, broke PASV, and was later removed; pasv_address was then temporarily removed as a troubleshooting experiment and restored after it did not help. Neither experiment remained. Additionally, verbose FTP debugging exposed the original password in captured output; the executor promptly rotated the password and synchronized both root-only handovers, so the exposed value was no longer current. The retained mode-0600 vendor configuration copy has a clear rollback purpose and is not treated as residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d3e18916d8ca4dd6","cmd-0dd88287f3634319","cmd-2787c20b3c2f4f5a","cmd-460e1b62773c42e9","cmd-05aa203700ce4958","cmd-bbeeae5230cf4469","cmd-f3293688c5674901","cmd-cca9e98d60e64c40","cmd-be0b80416a4044f9","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The mandatory restart protocol was also completed on both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart observation, restart acknowledgement and bounded disconnect, a changed boot identity independently corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service/data behavior. The main concern is operational hygiene from two reverted troubleshooting mutations and temporary disclosure of the subsequently rotated original password.","limitations":["Confinement was directly tested against traversal to /etc/passwd and writing at the jail root, but the evidence does not exhaustively probe every possible escape technique or host path.","Lifecycle snapshots are bounded inventories rather than complete filesystem or configuration diffs, so hygiene conclusions rely substantially on the complete captured command timeline and final audits.","The captured transcript retains the obsolete original password from verbose FTP debugging, although direct evidence shows it was immediately rotated and the current handovers were synchronized.","There is no cluster subsystem in this topology; restart recovery evidence therefore covers each node's applicable services, files, security state, and end-to-end FTP workflow rather than cluster membership or resynchronization."]}
